{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Behold, for peace.</b> When I was in the middle of my days, for he was thirty-nine years old when he fell ill. For if red bile overpowers a person's constitution, he will always be ill in his youth and at peace in his old age, and the reverse if moisture overpowers — and the middle years are years of peace." "<b>Bitter to me bitter.</b> Sometimes for emphasis. And some say from the root of תמורה (Leviticus 27:10 [sic])." "<b>From the pit of nothingness.</b> Some say from the root בלתי (Genesis 18:14 [sic]), and it is a noun on the pattern of פרי. Others said that in this place it is like לא, and it is the opposite. And the first is the straight one in my eyes, as if he said: You have clung to my soul and brought it up from the pit of nothingness." "<b>For You have cast me behind Your back.</b> By way of parable — like a person who does not see what is behind him. For we know that the Creator of the nations has no back; but this is proof for what our predecessors said: that on account of a sin he committed he was destined to die with shortened years. And they interpreted that one who dies before fifty-two years is בכרת (cut off). And over this he was grieved, for he did not know of any transgression he had committed that would obligate him to die by כרת. Therefore he said: Remember now how I walked before You and did what is good in Your eyes. And he mentioned both thought and deed. And the predecessors said that he did not engage in the commandment of procreation."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra cites Leviticus 38:17, but Leviticus has only 27 chapters; the word תמורה appears in Leviticus 27:10, which is likely the intended reference.", 2 "Ibn Ezra cites Genesis 18:12 but the word בלתי appears at Genesis 18:14; this may reflect a variant citation convention or a textual error in transmission.", 3 "Ibn Ezra cites Genesis 18:12 in comment 2 but the reference does not match; here in comment 3 the citation 'Leviticus 38:17' is impossible as Leviticus has 27 chapters — likely a corruption for Leviticus 27:28-29 regarding כרת, or another passage entirely."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:57:03.183561594Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}